"Civil Procedure is drawn as two horizontal bands facing each other across a gutter. The upper band, 'Can this court hear it?', is a skyline of four gates (authority over the defendant, notice, federal subject-matter jurisdiction, venue) standing left to right on one straight threshold line; each gate's height is its doctrinal depth, so personal jurisdiction towers at the left. The framework region sits at the top-left corner as the entry point, outside both bands. Hanging from the threshold line directly beneath personal jurisdiction, in the gutter, are the earlier jurisdiction landmarks: the historical root (Pennoyer to Shoe) from which the tallest gate grew, deliberately un-zoned so it reads as origin rather than as a stage. The lower band, 'The life of the suit', hangs from a single top line and flows left to right toward judgment: governing law (with the Berk v. Choy extension attached beneath it) under the framework, then pleading, joinder, class actions, and summary judgment as the terminus at the right edge. Columns align vertically where doctrine connects: the framework over Erie, subject-matter jurisdiction over joinder and class actions (supplemental jurisdiction, CAFA), so the ribbons between bands are short and mostly vertical. Two 'depends' spines from subject-matter jurisdiction down to Erie and to joinder show that the chamber rests on the gates; the flow spines trace the plaintiff's route."